= 1. Challenge of and Responses to Globalisation

In mid-1991, India abandoned four decades of state-dominated economic planning in response to a crippling macroeconomic crisis, embarking on structural integration with the global economy.

== The 1991 Balance of Payments Crisis

- *Exhaustion of Foreign Exchange Reserves:* By June 1991, India's foreign exchange reserves plummeted to approximately \USD 1USD 1.2 billion, barely sufficient to finance two weeks of essential imports. The sovereign was on the verge of defaulting on international debt obligations.
- *Underlying Macroeconomic Strains:* The crisis was triggered by persistent double-digit fiscal deficits throughout the 1980s, heavy reliance on short-term commercial borrowings, and the sudden outbreak of the Gulf War (1990–1991), which spiked international crude oil prices and halted vital foreign remittances from Indian expatriates in Kuwait and Iraq.
- *Emergency Sovereign Measures:* The caretaker government headed by Prime Minister Chandra Shekhar took the extraordinary emergency measure of physically airlifting 67 tonnes of national gold reserves to the Bank of England and the Union Bank of Switzerland to collateralize bilateral loans and avert a formal sovereign debt default.

== The New Economic Policy (NEP) & The Structural Reform Architecture

Following the May–June 1991 general elections, the newly installed Congress government headed by Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao, with distinguished economist Dr. Manmohan Singh as Finance Minister, unveiled historic structural reforms under the framework of *LPG (Liberalisation, Privatisation, Globalisation)*:

- *Liberalisation (Delicensing & Deregulation):* Abolished industrial licensing for almost all industries (except a handful relating to defense, hazardous chemicals, and security), dismantling the stifling "Licence-Permit-Quota Raj".
- *Privatisation (PSU Disinvestment):* Reduced the number of industries reserved exclusively for the public sector from 17 to just 3 (atomic energy, defense equipment, and railway transport); introduced partial disinvestment of government equity in Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs).
- *Globalisation (Trade & Capital Integration):* Slashed peak customs tariffs from over 300% to under 50%; relaxed foreign exchange controls through full current account convertibility of the Indian Rupee; raised Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) equity limits to 51% (and higher) across major sectors; and joined the World Trade Organization (WTO) as an original founding member on 1 January 1995.

== Multi-Dimensional Resistance to Globalisation

The structural economic shift encountered widespread ideological, labor, and grassroots opposition:

- *Trade Union & Organized Labor Opposition:* Central trade unions—principally the All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC) and Center of Indian Trade Unions (CITU)—organized nationwide general strikes protesting worker retrenchments, the closure of loss-making public enterprises, the casualization of contract labor, and the erosion of collective bargaining rights.
- *Agrarian & Farmers' Mobilization:*
  - *Karnataka Rajya Raitha Sangha (KRRS):* Led by the charismatic *Prof. M.D. Nanjundaswamy*, the KRRS launched militant direct actions against multinational agribusiness corporations, storming Kentucky Fried Chicken (KFC) outlets and destroying trial plots of genetically modified seeds.
  - *Resistance to WTO & TRIPS:* The Bharatiya Kisan Union (BKU) under Mahendra Singh Tikait and KRRS vehemently opposed the WTO Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights (TRIPS) agreement, rejecting the patenting of indigenous plants, seeds, and biological varieties (such as Neem and Basmati rice) by foreign multinational companies, while demanding the continuation of state agricultural subsidies on fertilizers, power, and irrigation.
- *Cultural & Swadeshi Resistance:* Intellectuals, left-wing cultural forums, and right-wing nationalist groups (such as the *Swadeshi Jagran Manch*) protested against the uninhibited influx of Western consumerism, commercialization of electronic mass media, and vulgarization of public culture, manifesting in protests against international beauty pageants and corporate branding.

