== Part 3: Secularism Models, Legal Cases & Contemporary Challenges

#mcq(11, [How does the Indian constitutional model of secularism fundamentally differ from the classic Western (American/French) model of secularism?], (
  [The Indian model completely outlaws all forms of private religious practice], [The Western model allows the state to manage temples and churches, while India does not], [The Indian model establishes a state-sponsored national religion], [The Indian model is characterized by 'principled distance' and equal respect for all religions (Sarva Dharma Sambhava) permitting state intervention for social reform, whereas the Western model mandates strict mutual exclusion and a rigid wall of separation],
))

#solution(11, [Indian secularism practices 'principled distance' and equal respect (Sarva Dharma Sambhava), allowing state intervention for progressive social reform, unlike the strict Western separation of church and state.], answer: [D])

#mcq(12, [The Shah Bano Case (1985), which sparked intense political debate over secularism and uniform civil laws, pertained to:], (
  [A divorced Muslim woman's constitutional right to claim maintenance from her husband under Section 125 of the Criminal Procedure Code], [A dispute over the ownership of religious waqf properties], [The introduction of reservations for religious minorities in government jobs], [The ban on triple talaq by an executive ordinance],
))

#solution(12, [In Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum (1985), the Supreme Court ruled that a divorced Muslim woman was entitled to maintenance under Section 125 of the CrPC, affirming gender justice.], answer: [A])

#mcq(13, [Which landmark legislation was enacted by Parliament in 1989 to prevent atrocities and crimes against members of the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, fulfilling a major demand of the Dalit movement?], (
  [The Protection of Civil Rights Act], [The Untouchability Offences Act], [The Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989], [The Maintenance of Internal Security Act],
))

#solution(13, [The SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989 was enacted to provide stringent legal penalties against caste violence, humiliation, and atrocities, fulfilling a central demand of Dalit movements.], answer: [C])

#mcq(14, [In the 1990s, which three Hindi heartland regional parties emerged as the primary vehicles of intermediate backward caste (OBC) political power?], (
  [DMK, AIADMK, and PMK], [Samajwadi Party (SP), Rashtriya Janata Dal (RJD), and Janata Dal (United) [JD(U)]], [Shiromani Akali Dal, National Conference, and TDP], [Asom Gana Parishad, BJD, and Trinamool Congress],
))

#solution(14, [The Samajwadi Party (UP), Rashtriya Janata Dal (Bihar), and Janata Dal (United) emerged as the dominant regional political parties articulating the interests of the agrarian backward classes.], answer: [B])

#mcq(15, [In the 2002 Godhra train incident, which tragedy served as the immediate trigger for widespread communal violence across the state of Gujarat?], (
  [A bomb blast in an Ahmedabad commercial market], [The demolition of a religious shrine in Vadodara], [A political assassination during an election rally], [The burning of coach S-6 of the Sabarmati Express at Godhra railway station carrying Karsevaks, resulting in the death of 59 persons],
))

#solution(15, [The burning of coach S-6 of the Sabarmati Express on 27 February 2002 at Godhra, which killed 59 people including Karsevaks, ignited retaliatory communal violence across Gujarat.], answer: [D])

